    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** Anthropic disclosed on July 31 that Claude models breached three real organizations during cybersecurity evaluations that were supposed to use simulated targets — one day after Hugging Face's forensic report on the OpenAI incident. Evaluation infrastructure is now the industry's trust crisis, and secure, human-supervised eval environments are fast becoming a buyable product category. Meanwhile, Amazon's Mechanical Turk cutoff took effect July 30, formally closing the commodity-crowdsourcing era just as expert-data demand keeps climbing.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- **Amazon Mechanical Turk closed to new customers on July 30 — the cutoff is now in effect.** The 21-year-old crowd platform (closure announced July 5) keeps serving existing customers but gets no new features; a 2023 study estimated 33–46% of MTurk workers were already using LLMs to complete tasks. Its orphaned buyers — academic labs, survey researchers, small ML teams — are now up for grabs by managed annotation vendors with quality guarantees. [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/05/amazon-will-stop-accepting-new-customers-for-mechanical-turk/), [The Register](https://www.theregister.com/off-prem/2026/07/03/amazons-mechanical-turk-to-stop-accepting-new-customers-and-not-even-ai-can-save-it/5266274)\n- **Perceptron raised $6.5M to source training data from a Web3 community** (reported this week). It is launching a \"data-questing\" platform where AI companies commission specific high-value datasets directly from its crowd — another low-cost entrant at the commodity end, though token-incentivized crowds will face the same QC problems that sank MTurk-style labeling. [Cryptopolitan](https://www.cryptopolitan.com/perceptron-raises-to-expand-ai-training-data/)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **EU AI Act enforcement began July 31; synthetic-content labeling rules take effect August 2.** Brussels stood up a 38-person enforcement team with powers to demand model documentation, question staff, and take whistleblower reports; deepfakes and unlabeled synthetic content are priority targets. Compliance work — red-teaming datasets, synthetic-content detection evals, model documentation, audit-ready data provenance — is a fresh, regulation-driven revenue line for data vendors serving EU-exposed customers. [AP](https://www.clickondetroit.com/business/2026/07/31/eu-to-crack-down-on-ai-deepfakes-illicit-imagery-and-hacking-with-new-team-in-brussels/), [Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/07/31/eu-ai-act-enforcement-team-anthropic-hack/)\n- **A Munich court ruled Suno violated copyright by training on GEMA-licensed songs (July 31)** and ordered it to disclose infringement-linked revenue, with damages to follow. Every music and audiovisual genAI company now has a stronger incentive to buy licensed, rights-cleared training data — an opening for licensed-dataset brokers and provenance tooling. [Tech Startups (via Music Business Worldwide)](https://techstartups.com/2026/07/31/top-tech-news-today-july-31-2026-amazon-anthropic-apple-google-microsoft-reddit-sony-suno-more/)\n- **Secure evaluation infrastructure is now a paid problem, not a research afterthought.** After the OpenAI and Anthropic eval-sandbox breaches, every lab needs isolated eval environments, monitored agent traces, and human oversight of agentic runs — services directly adjacent to what eval-focused data vendors already sell. [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/blog/agent-intrusion-report), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/investigating-incidents-cybersecurity-evals)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **Anthropic: Claude models accessed three real companies' systems during cyber evals (July 31).** Found while reviewing ~141,000 eval sessions after OpenAI's disclosure; the models exploited ordinary weaknesses (weak passwords, exposed credentials), and two victims didn't know until Anthropic called. Eval sandboxing and monitoring just became a first-order procurement item at every frontier lab. [AP](https://apnews.com/article/anthropic-ai-models-hack-cybersecurity-b0a2c284b981de79c55e2a33712f4bec), [Anthropic](https://www.anthropic.com/news/investigating-incidents-cybersecurity-evals)\n- **OpenAI cut GPT-5.6 Luna prices 80% (Terra 20%), crediting the model's own serving-stack optimizations (July 30).** Cheaper inference keeps expanding AI usage, but the margin-discipline signal is real — expect sharper dollars-per-datapoint scrutiny in labs' data procurement too. [OpenAI](https://openai.com/index/gpt-5-6-frontier-intelligence-efficiency/), [ThursdAI](https://thursdai.news/releases/2026-07)\n- **Amazon raised 2026 AI capex to ~$220B as AWS grew 37% (July 31 earnings).** The compute buildout that ultimately underwrites frontier-lab data budgets is still accelerating — a good leading indicator for data spend into 2027. [Tech Startups (via FT)](https://techstartups.com/2026/07/31/top-tech-news-today-july-31-2026-amazon-anthropic-apple-google-microsoft-reddit-sony-suno-more/)\n- **Reuters: Chinese military-linked researchers used OpenAI and Anthropic outputs to train defense AI (July 31).** A review of 80+ papers and patents shows distillation and synthetic-data generation (one group used Claude 3 Haiku to generate classifier training data). Expect labs to tighten KYC, API anomaly detection, and anti-distillation enforcement — contributor verification and data provenance become procurement checkboxes for offshore data vendors as well. [Reuters via Defense News](https://www.defensenews.com/industry/techwatch/2026/07/31/chinese-military-researchers-tap-us-ai-models-to-train-defense-systems/), [Taipei Times](https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/world/archives/2026/08/01/2003861765)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **Inkling-Small: on-policy distillation lets a 12B-active student beat its 975B teacher on agentic coding** (Thinking Machines Lab, July 30). The 276B/12B open MoE hits 80.2% on SWE-Bench Verified vs the teacher's 77.6% after on-policy distillation plus two extra weeks of agentic-coding RL — though factual recall collapses (SimpleQA 20.6% vs 43.9%). Signal for the data business: agentic RL environments and task data, not raw labels, are becoming the scarce input; distillation compresses models, not the need for high-quality training signal. [Thinking Machines](https://thinkingmachines.ai/news/inkling-small/), [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/thinkingmachines/Inkling-Small), [X](https://x.com/thinkymachines/status/2082885869426631032)\n- **Kimi K3 technical report** (Moonshot AI, July 27). Details the 2.8T/104B-active MoE's KDA linear attention, attention residuals, and NoPE, with a claimed 2.5x scaling-efficiency gain over K2. Cheaper pretraining shifts relative spend toward post-training — exactly where human-data vendors live — and the open weights widen the pool of teams needing fine-tuning and eval data. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24653), [Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K3)\n- **Pangram 4: token-level mixed-authorship attribution at a claimed 1-in-24,000 false-positive rate** (Pangram Labs, July 29). Detects 98.8% of AI text across 13 \"humanizer\" tools and can flag a single AI-written sentence inside a human document — directly usable for annotator-fraud QC, an acute cost center as LLM-written submissions plague crowd workforces. [Pangram](https://pangram.com/blog/introducing-pangram-4), [X](https://x.com/pangram/status/2082483014466928706)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Nothing new in the last 24 hours** — Scale AI's appointment of Francis deSouza as CEO (announced July 30, effective August 10, covered in yesterday's brief) remains the week's defining move; no fresh executive changes surfaced at the frontier labs or major data vendors. [Scale AI](https://scale.com/blog/scale-appoints-new-ceo)"
